# ============================ INVENTORY 3 ============================
LESSONS_I3 = [
L("Chapter 1 — Identity tracking: scope and record structure", "11", "<p>This module covers serial and batch operations in ZhiftERP. On completing this module you will be able to: configure items for batch or serial control with the full flag set; create and read Batch and Serial No records; move controlled items with their identity on every document; run expiry management on the standing reads; and execute the recall and freeze procedure that locates any batch across the network in minutes.</p><p><b>The two kinds of identity.</b></p><p>1. <b>Batch identity</b> — the lot: units made together share a batch number, an expiry date, and a fate. One Batch record covers many units. Pharmaceuticals, foods, chemicals — anything where the manufacturer's lot is the unit of quality and recall — run under batch control.<br>2. <b>Serial identity</b> — the unit: each piece has its own number and its own history. One Serial No record per unit. Equipment, devices, high-value items under warranty run under serial control.</p><p>An item can carry both (a serialised device manufactured in lots). The decision per item is Chapter 2's register.</p><p><b>What identity tracking buys, stated as capabilities.</b></p><p>1. <b>Location by identity</b> — the batch-wise balance answers where every unit of a lot sits, per warehouse, in one report.<br>2. <b>Expiry management</b> — expiry lives on the Batch record; the expiring-stock reads (Chapter 6) run from it.<br>3. <b>Recall execution</b> — a named batch is blocked, located, quarantined, and traced downstream in minutes (Chapter 7). For a pharmaceutical distributor this capability is a regulatory obligation, not a convenience.<br>4. <b>Unit history</b> — a serial number's full movement record: received when, sold to whom, returned when (Chapter 4).</p><p><b>The cost, stated honestly.</b> Identity tracking adds a step to every movement of a controlled item: the numbers must be captured, named, and confirmed. The discipline is front-loaded at the receiving dock (Chapter 5) — numbers entered once from the physical goods, then carried by the documents. A business that skips the dock discipline pays it back at every count, every recall drill, and every expiry write-off it saw too late.</p><p><b>Custody.</b> The stock function operates the records; the compliance owner (the superintendent pharmacist at a pharmaceutical business) owns the recall procedure and the disposal rules; the item-control decisions are made once per item class and recorded. The module 1 rule extends naturally: <b>nothing moves without its document, and for controlled items the document names the identity.</b></p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: item configuration. Chapter 3: the Batch record and the bundle mechanics. Chapter 4: the Serial No record. Chapter 5: movements with identity. Chapter 6: expiry management. Chapter 7: the recall and freeze procedure. Chapter 8: worked example. Chapter 9: review and the module rule.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — Item configuration for identity", "12", "<p>Identity control is switched on per item, on the Item record's inventory section. Walk the flags in order; each is set from the written control register, not per-item improvisation.</p><p><b>1. Has Batch No.</b> Tick to place the item under batch control: every stock movement of the item will require a batch number, and balances hold per batch.</p><p><b>2. Automatically Create New Batch</b> and <b>Batch Number Series.</b> Two receiving postures:</p><p>• <b>Manual batches (the pharmaceutical standard):</b> auto-create OFF — the receiver creates or selects the Batch record carrying the <b>manufacturer's own batch number</b> from the physical carton. The manufacturer's number is what a recall letter names, and a system-generated substitute would make Chapter 7's procedure a translation exercise under time pressure.<br>• <b>Auto batches:</b> auto-create ON with a series — for goods where the business's own lot identity suffices (internally produced lots, goods without manufacturer lots). The series is configured once per the naming rules.</p><p><b>3. Has Expiry Date</b> and <b>Shelf Life.</b> Tick where the item expires; the Batch record then requires an expiry date. Shelf Life (in days) lets the system propose expiry from manufacture date — a proposal the receiver corrects to the printed label, never a substitute for reading it.</p><p><b>4. Retain Sample</b> (where the site uses it) — the sample-retention quantity per batch for businesses whose quality process keeps reference samples; the retained stock sits in the sample warehouse the setting names.</p><p><b>5. Has Serial No</b> and <b>Serial Number Series.</b> Tick for unit-identity items. The series generates numbers where the business assigns its own; where the manufacturer's serial is the identity (devices), the receiver enters the physical numbers — the same manual-identity logic as batches.</p><p><b>6. Warranty Period.</b> On serialised items with warranty, the period in days: the Serial No record computes warranty expiry from delivery, read by the service flows.</p><p><b>The control register.</b> One page listing each item class, its flags, and the reason: <i>all NAFDAC-regulated pharmaceuticals — batch + expiry, manual batches (recall obligation); refrigerated lines — the same plus cold-chain warehouse defaults; equipment above the value threshold — serial + warranty.</i> New items inherit their class's row at creation (the item-creation checklist carries it), and an item created without its class's flags is caught by the monthly register read: controlled classes filtered against their flags, exceptions fixed before stock arrives — because <b>flags cannot be retro-fitted painlessly once un-batched stock exists</b>; the conversion of existing stock to batch control is a documented remediation, not a checkbox.</p><p><b>Verification.</b> Per new controlled item: the flags match the class row; the series or manual posture correct; shelf life entered where used; the first receipt observed end to end (Chapter 5's dock discipline proving the configuration).</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The Batch record and the bundle mechanics", "12", "<p>One Batch record per lot per item. This chapter covers the record and how documents carry batch identity.</p><p><b>1. The Batch record's fields.</b> Open Batch (awesome bar) &gt; New (or created at receipt per Chapter 2's posture):</p><p>• <b>Batch ID</b> — the manufacturer's batch number under the manual posture; the series value under auto-create. The ID is the record's name and prints everywhere.<br>• <b>Item</b> — one batch belongs to one item.<br>• <b>Manufacturing Date</b> and <b>Expiry Date</b> — from the physical label at receipt. The expiry date is the field Chapter 6's entire read structure hangs on; it is entered from the printed label, at the dock, by the receiver, and checked by the second signature on the receiving document.<br>• <b>Supplier reference fields</b> where the site adds them — the supplier's delivery reference against the lot, useful in recall correspondence.<br>• <b>Disabled</b> — the block switch: a disabled batch cannot post on new transactions. Chapter 7's freeze uses it.</p><p><b>2. Reading a batch.</b> The batch dashboard and the <b>batch-wise balance</b> view answer, per batch: quantity on hand per warehouse, and the movement history. This one lookup is the recall procedure's location step — from a batch number to the network-wide holdings in under a minute, which is the capability the whole module's discipline exists to keep true.</p><p><b>3. How documents carry batch identity — the bundle.</b> On stock documents, controlled rows carry their identity through the <b>Serial and Batch Bundle</b>: the row references a bundle record listing the batch (or serial) numbers and quantities that make up the row's total. Mechanics the operator needs:</p><p>1. One row can draw from several batches — the bundle lists each batch and its quantity; the row's total equals the bundle's sum.<br>2. The bundle is created on the document (the row's identity picker) and stands as the row's identity record; the ledger entries post per batch from it.<br>3. Reports and traces read through bundles: the question of which deliveries carried batch B-1147 is answered by the bundles that name it (Chapter 7's downstream trace).</p><p><b>4. Batch selection at issue and delivery.</b> When stock leaves, the picker proposes available batches. The selection rule is the written policy's — for expiring goods, <b>first-expiry-first-out</b>: the earliest-expiry batch ships first, and the picker's expiry visibility makes the rule executable at the bench. The configuration options that automate or default the selection are set per the policy; the operator's rule stands either way: the batch on the document is the batch physically picked — a document naming batch A over goods picked from batch B is an identity error that surfaces at the next count or the next recall, whichever is more expensive.</p><p><b>5. Batch hygiene.</b> Monthly, with the stock reads: zero-balance expired batches disabled; batches with residual fractions investigated (a 0.5-unit batch balance is usually a UoM or repack arithmetic error); the repack inheritance check — produced retail units carrying their source batch identity per module 2's register (the produced strips of a carton belong to the carton's manufacturer lot, because the recall letter will not distinguish forms).</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — The Serial No record", "12", "<p>One Serial No record per unit. This chapter covers the record, capture, and the unit-history reads.</p><p><b>1. The Serial No record's fields.</b> Open Serial No (awesome bar) — normally created at receipt, read thereafter:</p><p>• <b>Serial No</b> — the unit's number: the manufacturer's serial under manual capture, the series value where the business assigns its own.<br>• <b>Item</b> — the unit's item.<br>• <b>Status and location</b> — the record shows where the unit stands: in which warehouse, delivered, or otherwise disposed; maintained by the documents that move it.<br>• <b>Warranty fields</b> — the warranty expiry computed from the item's period and the delivery date; the amc/maintenance fields where the service flows use them.<br>• <b>The movement history</b> — the unit's document trail: received on which Purchase Receipt, transferred on which entries, delivered on which Delivery Note. The history is the answer to every unit-level question — the warranty claim, the theft report, the customer dispute — and it is complete exactly to the degree Chapter 5's discipline held.</p><p><b>2. Capture at receipt.</b> Serial numbers enter the system once, at the dock:</p><p>1. The receiver scans or types each unit's number from the physical unit (not from the supplier's packing list — the list states intent; the units state fact, and the two differ often enough to make the rule).<br>2. The receipt row's bundle lists the serials; the count of serials equals the row quantity or the row does not submit.<br>3. Discrepancies (a duplicate number, a number already in the system) stop the line at the dock — a duplicate serial is either a supplier error to bounce or a data error to fix now, and both are cheaper standing at the dock than discovered at delivery.</p><p><b>3. Verification at delivery.</b> Serials leave against documents exactly as they entered: the Delivery Note's bundle names the units shipped, confirmed against the physical units at picking. The delivered serial is what the warranty record, the customer's file, and any later dispute stand on.</p><p><b>4. The serial reads.</b></p><p>1. <b>The location census</b> (quarterly, or monthly for high-value classes): the Serial No list filtered to in-stock status per warehouse, sampled against the shelf — five units picked from the list and found physically, five picked from the shelf and found in the list. Identity accuracy is proven both directions, in ten minutes.<br>2. <b>The status hygiene read:</b> serials in impossible states — in-stock in a disabled warehouse, delivered against no document — each an incident to trace, because a serial record that disagrees with its own documents means a movement bypassed the bundle.<br>3. <b>The warranty horizon</b> (where service flows run): units whose warranty expires in the next quarter, to the service owner.</p><p><b>5. Serial-and-batch items.</b> Items carrying both controls capture both at the dock; the bundle rows carry the serial with its batch. The recall trace (Chapter 7) then resolves to named units — the strongest identity the system offers, purchased with the strictest dock discipline.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Movements with identity", "12", "<p>Every module 2 movement, re-run for controlled items: the identity travels on the document. This chapter is the per-document discipline.</p><p><b>1. The receiving dock — where identity is born.</b> The Purchase Receipt of a controlled item is the module's most important fifteen minutes:</p><p>1. <b>Batches:</b> per lot on the pallet — the Batch record created or selected with the manufacturer's number, the expiry entered from the printed label, the bundle listing batch and quantity. Mixed-lot deliveries are split honestly: three lots on one line is three bundle entries, not one batch chosen for convenience.<br>2. <b>Serials:</b> Chapter 4's capture — physical units, count equals quantity, discrepancies stopped at the dock.<br>3. <b>The second signature:</b> the receiving check (the Procurement track's receiving controls) extends to identity — the checker confirms batch numbers and expiries against labels, because a wrong expiry entered at the dock mis-schedules every downstream read for the life of the lot.</p><p><b>2. Transfers.</b> The despatch entry's rows carry their bundles; the receiving site confirms <b>identity, not just quantity</b> — 40 cartons arriving is half the check; 40 cartons of the named batch is the check. A transfer received against the wrong batch corrupts both sites' batch balances while the totals look perfect, which is why the receiving procedure names the batch comparison explicitly.</p><p><b>3. Issues.</b> Damage, expiry, and write-off issues of controlled items name their batches — the expiry write-off in particular is a per-batch document (Chapter 6's lane), because the disposal record the compliance owner keeps is batch-level and the two must cite each other.</p><p><b>4. Repack.</b> Module 2's inheritance rule, restated as the mechanic: the produced rows' bundles carry the identity the conversion register assigns — for pharmaceutical carton-to-strip conversion, the source manufacturer batch, so the lot's recall reach includes every form it was sold in. The repack entry that produces an anonymous batch has cut the recall trace at exactly the point a regulator will ask about.</p><p><b>5. Sales and deliveries.</b> The Delivery Note and POS sale of batched goods carry the batch on the row (the ZhiftPOS track covers the till's batch prompt). The delivered batch is the downstream trace's raw material: Chapter 7's who-received-it question is answered by delivery bundles, and it is answerable exactly where this discipline held.</p><p><b>6. Counts.</b> Module 4's counts of controlled items count <b>per batch</b> — the count sheet lists batch lines, and the identity audit rides the count: expiries on labels re-checked against Batch records, unlisted batches on the shelf flagged (stock present under a batch the system never received is a receiving bypass surfacing). The count is where dock-discipline debt comes due; a clean identity count is the cheapest evidence the whole module is working.</p><p><b>The one-line summary of the chapter:</b> identity is captured once, from the physical goods, at the boundary — and after that, every document names it and every handover confirms it. There is no later, cheaper place to fix identity than the dock.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Expiry management", "12", "<p>Expiry management is arithmetic on Batch expiry dates plus three lanes. This chapter is the reads and the lanes.</p><p><b>1. The data precondition.</b> Every read below computes from the Expiry Date on Batch records, entered at the dock from printed labels (Chapters 3, 5). The reads are exactly as good as that field; the monthly identity audit riding the counts (Chapter 5) is what keeps it good.</p><p><b>2. The expiry reads, by horizon.</b></p><p>1. <b>The expired list (weekly):</b> batches past expiry with balance on hand, per warehouse. Target: zero — expired stock should have left the trading warehouses through the lanes below before its date. Every line on this list is a lane that did not run in time, and the line's age is the process gap's size.<br>2. <b>The 90-day list (monthly, with the close):</b> batches expiring within ninety days, with quantities, values, and locations. This is the working list — the horizon at which the near-expiry lanes below still have room to act. The ninety-day window is the policy's number; set it to the longest lane's lead time (a return-to-vendor negotiation takes weeks; a markdown programme takes a sell-through cycle).<br>3. <b>The horizon profile (quarterly, to the owner):</b> stock value by expiry band — beyond a year, six-to-twelve months, ninety days to six months, inside ninety. The profile is purchasing feedback: a fattening short band means buying is outrunning sell-through, and the figure goes to the procurement owner as data with the affected items named.</p><p><b>3. The near-expiry lanes.</b> Per the written policy, in preference order:</p><p>1. <b>Accelerated sale</b> — the markdown per the concession rules (the ZhiftPOS track's markdown machinery, with the near-expiry reason code), moving the stock while it is lawful to sell, at a price the policy sets by remaining life.<br>2. <b>Return to vendor</b> — where supplier terms allow near-expiry returns: the Procurement track's return flow, initiated inside the terms' window, with the batch named on the return.<br>3. <b>Inter-branch rebalancing</b> — the batch moved to the site that sells it fastest (module 7's sell-through data names the site), through the standard transfer with its identity.</p><p><b>4. The expired lane.</b> Stock that reaches expiry:</p><p>1. <b>Blocked from sale</b> — the expiry controls stop expired batches from posting on outgoing sales documents per the site's configuration, and the batch is disabled per Chapter 3's hygiene where the block needs reinforcing.<br>2. <b>Moved to quarantine by document</b> — off the pickable balance, awaiting disposal.<br>3. <b>Written off through the damage/expiry lane</b> (module 2, Chapter 3): the issue naming the batch, the value to the expiry account, the approval per the ladder, and the disposal per the regulatory procedure the compliance owner runs — the stock issue and the disposal record citing each other.</p><p><b>5. The monthly expiry meeting note.</b> One paragraph with the close: the expired list's state (target zero, exceptions aged and explained), the 90-day list's total and its three largest lines with their chosen lanes, and the write-off total for the month against its approvals. Expiry losses are decided expenses; the note is how they stay decided rather than discovered.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The recall and freeze procedure", "13", "<p>A recall names a batch; the business must stop it, find it, and account for it — fast. This chapter is the written procedure, executable by whoever is on duty.</p><p><b>1. The trigger.</b> A regulator's letter, a manufacturer's notice, or the business's own quality finding names an item and a batch (or a batch range). The procedure starts the moment the notice is read; the steps below are sequenced for speed, and each has a time target.</p><p><b>2. Step 1 — freeze (target: fifteen minutes from notice).</b></p><p>1. Open the Batch record; set <b>Disabled</b> — new transactions on the batch are blocked network-wide from that moment: no till can sell it, no transfer can move it, at every site simultaneously. This single switch is why manual manufacturer batch numbers matter (Chapter 2): the letter's number is the record's ID, found in one search.<br>2. Notify the sites: one message to the keepers naming the item and batch, instructing physical segregation pending the transfers below.</p><p><b>3. Step 2 — locate (target: minutes, not hours).</b> The batch-wise balance for the batch: quantity per warehouse, network-wide, including transit and subcontract holdings. Print it — this snapshot is the recall file's opening page and the reconciliation base for everything recovered.</p><p><b>4. Step 3 — quarantine (same day).</b> Per site with holdings: a Material Transfer of the batch's balance to the site's quarantine warehouse (module 1's specials doing their job), the physical stock moved and shelf-checked against the batch balance. The transfer documents are the recall file's custody evidence.</p><p><b>5. Step 4 — the downstream trace (same day).</b></p><p>1. <b>Deliveries and sales:</b> the documents whose bundles name the batch — which customers received it, in what quantities, on which dates. The customer-notification list per the regulator's instruction builds from this trace.<br>2. <b>The produced-from trace:</b> repack entries citing the batch (module 2's register and Chapter 5's inheritance) — every form the lot was converted into joins the recall with its own locate-and-quarantine pass. The trace is complete exactly where the inheritance discipline held.<br>3. <b>Returns handling:</b> recalled units returning from customers are received into quarantine by document, against the notification list.</p><p><b>6. Step 5 — the account and the file.</b> The recall file: the notice, the freeze time, the location snapshot, the quarantine transfers, the downstream trace with the notification list, the returns received, and the final disposition (return to manufacturer or destruction per the notice, through the appropriate documented lane, values to the accounts the case rules). The file answers the regulator's follow-up in one folder — the standing one-lookup standard, at its highest stakes.</p><p><b>7. The drill.</b> Twice a year, the compliance owner names a batch and the procedure runs for real up to the quarantine step (transfers reversed after). The drill's two metrics — notice-to-freeze time, freeze-to-located time — are recorded, and every drill finding (the site that missed the message, the repack whose inheritance broke) is fixed at its source. A recall procedure that has never run is a document; the drill is what makes it a capability.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Worked example: the B-1147 recall at HealthTrade", "13", "<p>This chapter runs the Chapter 7 procedure on a real morning. Values and timings are listed per step.</p><p><b>The trigger.</b> A regulator's letter arrives at 08:40 on a Tuesday: a manufacturer-level quality recall naming PARA-500, batch <b>B-1147</b>.</p><p><b>Step 1 — freeze, 08:40-08:55.</b> The stock manager opens Batch B-1147 — one search, because receiving discipline stored the manufacturer's own number — and sets Disabled. All three sites see the block at once: the Ibadan till's attempt to sell a strip of the batch at 09:10 is refused by the system before the branch has finished reading the notification message. The keeper message goes out at 08:52 naming the item, the batch, and the segregation instruction. Notice-to-freeze: fifteen minutes.</p><p><b>Step 2 — locate, by 09:05.</b> The batch-wise balance prints the network snapshot: <b>34 cartons at Central, 12 at Ibadan, 0 at Akure</b> (Akure's PARA-500 stock was always a different lot — the batch balance proves it in one line, sparing Akure the shelf sweep), <b>0 in transit</b>. The snapshot files as page one.</p><p><b>Step 3 — quarantine, same morning.</b> Central: one transfer, 34 cartons to <i>Central - Quarantine</i>, shelf-checked. Ibadan: 12 cartons to a segregated, labelled area per the branch procedure and transferred in the system to the quarantine designation the site uses. Both transfers file as custody evidence.</p><p><b>Step 4 — the downstream trace, same day.</b></p><p>1. The delivery and sales documents citing B-1147 list the wholesale customers who received the batch and the quantities; the notification list builds from it and goes out per the letter's instruction.<br>2. The produced-from trace: module 2's repack register shows the standing carton-to-strip conversion, and the repack entries citing B-1147 account for <b>88 retail strips at Central produced from the batch</b> — the strips inherit the recall, are located by the same batch balance (the inheritance discipline paying at the exact moment it exists for), and join the quarantine transfer.<br>3. Returns: two wholesale customers return their holdings over the following week, received into quarantine against the notification list.</p><p><b>Step 5 — the account.</b> The final disposition per the manufacturer's instruction: the quarantined stock returns to the manufacturer, documented through the return flow with the batch named, the credit handled by the Procurement track's lanes. The recall file closes: notice, timings, snapshot, transfers, trace, notification list, returns, disposition — one folder, and the regulator's follow-up inspection three weeks later is answered from it in one sitting.</p><p><b>The findings, flat.</b> Two process notes from the live run, both fixed within the week: the Ibadan keeper message went to the keeper's personal line rather than the duty channel (the notification list on the procedure corrected to the duty channel); and one wholesale delivery from the trace pre-dated batch capture on that customer's document type (a configuration gap from the go-live era — the affected document class confirmed batch-mandatory, and the customer contacted from the receipt-date range instead). The drill calendar adopts the real run's timings as its baseline: fifteen minutes to freeze, twenty-five to located.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common errors and the module rule", "11", "<p>The errors that recur in identity operations, each with its correction and prevention.</p><p><b>Error 1 — system batches over manufacturer lots.</b> Symptom: auto-created batch numbers on regulated goods; a recall letter naming a number the system has never heard of; the freeze step becoming a research project. Correction: the posture switched to manual capture; existing lots re-identified by remediation where feasible. Prevention: the control register's posture per class — manual manufacturer numbers wherever a recall can name them.</p><p><b>Error 2 — expiry from the packing list.</b> Symptom: expiry dates entered from paperwork rather than labels; the expiry reads mis-scheduled for the life of every mis-entered lot. Correction: the identity audit re-checking labels against Batch records; corrections documented. Prevention: the dock rule — dates from the printed label, confirmed by the second signature.</p><p><b>Error 3 — identity unconfirmed at handover.</b> Symptom: transfers received on quantity alone; batch balances wrong at both sites while totals reconcile. Correction: the affected batches recounted per site and corrected by document. Prevention: the receiving procedure's explicit batch comparison — identity, not just quantity.</p><p><b>Error 4 — the broken inheritance.</b> Symptom: repacks producing anonymous or new batches; the recall trace stopping at the conversion; retail stock unreachable by the lot that made it. Correction: produced batches re-linked to sources where records allow; the register's inheritance rule enforced. Prevention: module 2's conversion register carrying the batch handling per conversion, and the monthly repack read checking it.</p><p><b>Error 5 — the paper procedure.</b> Symptom: a recall procedure that exists as a document and has never run; the first live recall spent learning the tools. Correction: the drill run now. Prevention: the twice-yearly drill with recorded timings and source-fixed findings.</p><p><b>Error 6 — expired stock on the shelf.</b> Symptom: the expired list carrying aged lines; expired goods pickable; write-offs discovered rather than decided. Correction: the expired lane run — quarantine, write-off with approvals, disposal per compliance. Prevention: the weekly expired read at target zero and the 90-day working list feeding the near-expiry lanes in time.</p><p><b>Review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: state the two identities and what each buys; configure an item from the control register — batch posture, expiry, shelf life, serials, warranty — and state why flags precede stock; create and read Batch records, the batch-wise balance, and the bundle mechanics including multi-batch rows and first-expiry-first-out; operate Serial No capture at receipt, verification at delivery, and the location census; run every movement with identity — the dock discipline, transfer confirmation, per-batch issues, repack inheritance, delivery bundles, per-batch counts; run the expiry reads and lanes on their cadences; and execute the recall procedure to its time targets with the drill.</p><p><b>Module rule 3: identity travels with the stock — captured once from the physical goods at the dock, named on every movement and confirmed at every handover, expiries read on their horizons, and any batch freezable and locatable across the network in minutes.</b> Module 4 covers stock reconciliation and counting.</p>"),
]

